parts.text:
Now add tests for streams and createReadStream/WriteStream, then run tests.

Test plan:
```kotlin
@Test
fun fsStreamsAndStreamModule() {
    val workspace = Workspace.at(tmp.newFolder())
    val result = run(
        workspace,
        """
        var fs = require('fs');
        var stream = require('stream');
        var ws = fs.createWriteStream('out.txt');
        ws.on('finish', function () {
          var rs = fs.createReadStream('out.txt');
          var got = '';
          rs.on('data', function (c) { got += c.toString(); });
          rs.on('end', function () {
            console.log('read=' + got);
            var pt = new stream.PassThrough();
            var acc = '';
            pt.on('data', function (c) { acc += c.toString(); });
            pt.on('end', function () { console.log('through=' + acc); });
            pt.write('abc');
            pt.end('def');
          });
        });
        ws.write('hello ');
        ws.end('world');
        """.trimIndent(),
    )
    assertEquals("stderr: ${result.stderr}", 0, result.exitCode)
    assertTrue("stdout: ${result.stdout}", result.stdout.contains("read=hello world"))
    assertTrue("stdout: ${result.stdout}", result.stdout.contains("through=abcdef"))
}
```

Wait: createWriteStream truncates at creation, writes append. On finish, read. Should be "hello world".

But there's an ordering concern: createWriteStream schedules setImmediate to emit 'open'. The write('hello ') is called synchronously right after, appends to file (fsAppend creates file). Then end('world') appends then finalize → emit finish. All synchronous in main script! Actually bridge calls are synchronous. So by the time main script finishes, file contains "hello world", and finish already emitted (synchronously during end()). The finish listener (added before writes) runs during end() call, which reads file synchronously and emits 'data' asynchronously via setImmediate. Fine; ticks run after main. Output should appear.

Wait, `fs.createReadStream` reads file in setImmediate. Good.

The PassThrough: `pt.write('abc')` triggers _write→_transform→push('abc'). Since no data listener yet? We added `pt.on('data')` before write. on triggers resume → flowing. push emits via _drainReadable setImmediate. Then pt.end('def') writes then finalizes, emitting finish. The buffered 'abc' and 'def' drain in setImmediate. Then push(null) on end? Actually Transform: when writable side ends, does Readable side get push(null)? In Node, Transform emits 'end' on readable side when writable finishes. My implementation never calls `this.push(null)` for Transform. So `pt.on('end')` never fires! Need to handle: Transform should end its readable side when writable finishes (after _final). Let me add push(null) in Transform after finalize.

In `Transform.prototype._final`, after flush, call `self.push(null)`. And if no flushTransform, still push(null) after cb. Let me restructure:

```js
Transform.prototype._final = function (cb) {
  var self = this;
  function finish(err, out) {
    if (out !== undefined && out !== null) { self.push(out); }
    self.push(null);
    cb(err);
  }
  if (typeof this._flushTransform !== 'function') { return finish(); }
  this._flushTransform(function (err, out) { finish(err, out); });
};
```

But careful: `push(null)` sets ended and calls _drainReadable; the 'end' event fires after buffered data drained. In `_drainReadable`, it first drains buffer (flowing), then `_maybeEnd`. Good.

